.TH REFFECTOR 1 "2026-10-16" "reffector" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
reffector \- report statically reachable Go side effects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B reffector
.RI [ flags ]
.RI [ packages ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B reffector
analyzes Go source without running its functions, initializers, or tests.
It reports recognized externally observable operations reachable from selected
functions, directly or through calls. It does not modify analyzed source and
does not perform refactoring.
.PP
Packages use Go package-pattern semantics through
.BR golang.org/x/tools/go/packages .
Examples include
.BR . ,
.BR ./billing ,
.BR ./... ,
.BR ./internal/... ,
and import paths. No arguments select
.BR . .
Dependencies are inspected for propagation but normally are not displayed.
The current Go build environment selects files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -direct
Report only effects directly attributed to each function.
.TP
.B -tests
Include functions defined in internal and external test files. Generated test
executables are not displayed or run.
.TP
.B -json
Write function-summary JSON instead of text pairs.
.TP
.BR -h , " " -help
Print usage and exit successfully.
.SH OUTPUT
Default output contains sorted, deduplicated function/effect pairs:
.PP
.nf
example.com/app.Load fs.read
example.com/app.(*Store).Save db.exec
.fi
.PP
Methods include their receiver type. Closures use suffixes such as
.BR $1 .
Functions with no recognized effects are omitted from text output.
.PP
JSON is an array of function summaries. Each has full
.B package
import path,
.B function
identity,
.B effects
array, and boolean
.BR uncertain .
Each effects item has an
.B identifier
and boolean
.BR direct .
An empty effects array is valid. Uncertainty is separate from effects: it means
an unresolved or opaque call is reachable. A function with no known effect but
uncertainty is visible only in JSON. An empty effects array, or absence from
text output, never proves purity.
.SH SIDE EFFECTS
The supported vocabulary is:
.BR fs.read ,
.BR fs.write ,
.BR fs.create ,
.BR fs.remove ,
.BR net.connect ,
.BR net.listen ,
.BR process.exec ,
.BR process.exit ,
.BR env.read ,
.BR env.write ,
.BR db.query ,
and
.BR db.exec .
They respectively describe filesystem reads, writes, creation and removal;
network connection and listening; external-process start and os.Exit process
termination; environment reads and writes; and database query and command
operations.
.PP
Summaries are package- and receiver-qualified, never inferred from a method
name. The table covers common APIs in os (including File and Root),
path/filepath, io/ioutil, net, crypto/tls, net/http, os/exec, and database/sql.
Basic-argument fmt.Sprintf, nil-qualifier go/types.TypeString, and bytes.Buffer
methods are known effect-free boundaries. Formatting or qualifier callbacks
that can execute application code are uncertain instead. os.File.Write is
classified fs.write, though a descriptor can refer to a pipe, device, or
standard output. File.Close has no filesystem-write classification.
.PP
When os.OpenFile has a constant flags argument, read-only access reports
fs.read and write/create flags report only applicable effects. Unknown flags
conservatively report reading, writing, and creation.
.SH DIRECT AND TRANSITIVE EFFECTS
A call to a summarized API is direct. Effects of ordinary application helpers
are inherited. For example, if Load calls os.ReadFile and Process calls Load,
both report fs.read normally, while
.B -direct
reports it only for Load. Recursive cycles converge through a monotone
worklist.
.PP
Direct effects do not make unresolved behavior certain. JSON uncertainty is
propagated to callers independently of the effect set.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
reffector .
reffector ./...
reffector -direct ./internal/...
reffector -tests -json ./billing
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
A result is possible static reachability, not actual execution. The analyzer
follows static calls, methods, closures, known local function values, phi
merges, instantiated generics, goroutines, defers, and calls across packages.
It handles a known concrete interface receiver by following that method.
.PP
It intentionally does not use class-hierarchy expansion for an open interface
receiver. An interface parameter or memory-loaded interface does not establish
that every loaded implementation is a callee; such a call is marked uncertain.
This avoids speculative effects such as assigning an arbitrary io.Writer call
to os.File.Write, but may miss effects of the real receiver. Function values
flowing through memory, parameters, or returns are also uncertain.
.PP
Reflection, cgo, assembly, missing Go bodies, unknown standard-library calls,
and behavior outside summaries are uncertain. Branch feasibility, paths, URLs,
resource identities, permissions, shared-memory mutation, plugin code,
secondary callbacks or drivers, and all build tags/platforms are not modeled.
Uncertainty does not enumerate every possible missing effect.
.PP
Module loading uses -mod=readonly. It copies workspace metadata to temporary
files so source and workspace checksum files are not modified. The Go toolchain
may still update its caches; this command is not a sandbox for untrusted tools.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful analysis, including no reported effects and help.
.TP
.B 1
Package loading, analysis, or output failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line flags.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR go (1),
.BR go-test (1),
.BR go-vet (1),
.BR man (7)
.PP
https://pkg.go.dev/golang.org/x/tools/go/packages
